# A synthesizer's voice: the signal generator's square wave through a transistor ladder filter, the
# circuit Robert Moog patented in 1969, and out through a speaker. Press S1 to play a note.
#
# **S1 strikes the note.** It charges CE in about 10 ms, and let go CE drains through RD over about
# a second. That voltage, through REM, opens QT: the ladder's current rises at once and dies away with
# it, and the ladder's corner and its gain both follow the current, so each note starts bright and loud
# and fades darker and quieter — the Moog's pluck. Tap out a bass line; the generator's panel sets the
# pitch, and its waveform: the square is hollow and woody, the sawtooth, every harmonic at once, the
# brassy sound most people think of as a Moog.
#
# The ladder is ten transistors. Q1 and Q2 at the bottom are a differential pair, steering the current
# QT gives them between the two columns as the input swings; above them four rungs of pairs, Q3 to
# Q10, pass the current up, each rung's bases held still by its own battery. At every rung the current
# meets a capacitor on each side, CnA and CnB, and every rung is one pole of a low-pass filter whose
# corner is the current over the capacitor: four poles, so above the corner the square's harmonics
# fall away 24 dB an octave, the Moog's sound.
#
# **P1 is where the note rests.** It also sets QT's base, under the envelope: at its 0.5 V QT is off
# between notes and the ladder silent. Turn it up for a drone that never closes: about 0.85 V is a few
# hundred hertz, dark and muffled; 2.5 V is a few kilohertz, bright.
#
# **RK is the resonance**: the output fed back to Q2, the pair's other side. Around the corner the four
# poles turn the signal half way round, so what comes back there adds rather than subtracts, and the
# filter rings at its corner as it sweeps — the vowel-like sweep of a synthesizer. How hard it rings
# is the loop's gain: RB2 / (RB2 + RK) of the output back, times the ladder's own gain, which follows
# its current. At four the ladder sings on its own; 680k puts a struck note's loop at about three, so
# each edge of the square rings once and alike, the ringing fading with the note as its current does.
# Past four, what came back would drive the input pair from one side to the other, and each falling
# edge would spike down far further than the rising edge went up. The input is kept to 10 mV for the
# same reason: the pair is straight only within a few tens of millivolts, and a louder square bends the
# two edges apart.
#
# **U1 hears the two columns' difference**, as the Moog's own output stage does. When a note is struck
# both columns fall by volts together as the ladder's current comes on; heard from one column, that
# fall is a thump at the start of every note, and as a difference it cancels, while the note itself,
# opposite in the two, doubles. CIN and CSP are large so that neither the generator's coupling nor the
# speaker's lets a half of the square sag before the next edge. The speaker plays while the clock runs at
# a second a second, and the status line says how fast the sound is being worked out: this is the
# heaviest circuit here, about 40 unknowns solved some 30,000 times a second of sound, and on an idle
# machine it keeps up with about one and a half times to spare.

speed 1
start settled

battery   BV    40 0  40 8   15V
ground          40 8
wire            14 0  40 0

# The loads at the top of the ladder.
resistor  RLA   14 0  14 4   47k
resistor  RLB   20 0  20 4   47k

# Four rungs, each pair's bases tied and held by its battery, and a capacitor each side.
npn       Q9    14 4  16 6   14 8   2N3904
npn       Q10   20 4  18 6   20 8   2N3904
wire            16 6  18 6
battery   BB4   17 6  17 8   8V
ground          17 8
capacitor C4A   14 8  11 8   220n
ground          11 8
capacitor C4B   20 8  23 8   220n
ground          23 8

npn       Q7    14 8  16 10  14 12  2N3904
npn       Q8    20 8  18 10  20 12  2N3904
wire            16 10 18 10
battery   BB3   17 10 17 12  7V
ground          17 12
capacitor C3A   14 12 11 12  220n
ground          11 12
capacitor C3B   20 12 23 12  220n
ground          23 12

npn       Q5    14 12 16 14  14 16  2N3904
npn       Q6    20 12 18 14  20 16  2N3904
wire            16 14 18 14
battery   BB2   17 14 17 16  6V
ground          17 16
capacitor C2A   14 16 11 16  220n
ground          11 16
capacitor C2B   20 16 23 16  220n
ground          23 16

npn       Q3    14 16 16 18  14 20  2N3904
npn       Q4    20 16 18 18  20 20  2N3904
wire            16 18 18 18
battery   BB1   17 18 17 20  5V
ground          17 20
capacitor C1A   14 20 11 20  220n
ground          11 20
capacitor C1B   20 20 23 20  220n
ground          23 20

# The input pair and the current it steers.
npn       Q1    14 20 12 22  14 24  2N3904
npn       Q2    20 20 22 22  20 24  2N3904
wire            14 24 20 24
npn       QT    17 24 19 27  17 28  2N3904
resistor  RT    17 28 17 32  10k
ground          17 32
resistor  RCV   19 27 30 27  10k
supply    P1    30 27 30 31  0.5 10m

# The envelope: S1 charges CE through RA in about 10 ms, and let go, CE drains through RD over about
# half a second. Through REM it lifts QT's base above what P1 holds it at.
wire            19 27 19 30
resistor  REM   19 30 19 34  47k
capacitor CE    19 34 19 38  10u
ground          19 38
wire            19 34 21 34
resistor  RD    21 34 21 38  47k
ground          21 38
resistor  RA    21 34 23 34  1k
button    S1    23 34 26 34
battery   BE    26 34 26 38  15V
ground          26 38
ground          30 31

# The input: the generator onto Q1, both bases biased to 4 V, which leaves QT room to stay out of
# saturation at the top of P1's range.
gen       G1    4 22  4 26   square 110 10m
ground          4 26
capacitor CIN   4 22  8 22   10u
wire            8 22  12 22
resistor  RB1   12 22 12 26  10k
battery   BIN1  12 26 12 30  4V
ground          12 30
wire            22 22 24 22
resistor  RB2   24 22 28 22  10k
battery   BIN2  28 22 28 26  4V
ground          28 26

# The resonance: the output back to Q2.
resistor  RK    24 22 24 18  680k
capacitor CK    24 18 24 15  10u
wire            24 15 24 2
wire            24 2  34 2
wire            34 2  34 6

# U1 hears the two columns' difference, about BREF's 7.5 V: the right column's top through RDA, half
# of it against BREF through RIB, and the left column's top through RDB against RF.
wire            20 4  21 4
resistor  RDA   21 4  23 4   100k
wire            23 4  27 4
wire            27 4  27 7
wire            27 7  28 7
resistor  RIB   27 7  27 10  100k
battery   BREF  27 10 27 13  7.5V
ground          27 13
opamp     U1    30 6  TL072
wire            30 4  30 0
ground          30 8
wire            14 4  12 4
wire            12 4  12 1
wire            12 1  25 1
resistor  RDB   25 1  28 1   100k
wire            28 1  28 3
wire            28 5  28 3
resistor  RF    28 3  32 3   100k
wire            32 3  32 6

# The speaker.
wire            32 6  34 6
capacitor CSP   34 6  36 6   100u
resistor  RS    36 6  36 10  1k
speaker   SPK   36 10 36 13  8
ground          36 13

label     voice 34 6
meter     VOICE 36 10 36 13
